course: french
lesson: 17
title: Clothes
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Some clothes words are traps for English speakers: une veste is a jacket, not a vest, des baskets are trainers, not baskets, and une robe is a dress, not a dressing gown.
explain: English says "trousers" as if there were two, but French says one: un pantalon, and the same goes for un jean and un short.
explain: Word endings give hints about gender: words ending in -eau, like un chapeau (a hat) and un manteau (a coat), are almost always masculine, and words ending in -ette, like une casquette (a cap), almost always feminine.
explain: Colours still agree with clothes: black shoes are des chaussures noires, with an e because chaussure is feminine and an s because there are two.
explain: Picture a cold morning in Strasbourg: the host mum says Mets ton manteau, il fait froid !, "Put your coat on, it's cold!", just like grown-ups everywhere.
fact: un pantalon ("pahn-ta-LON") is one pair of trousers and is singular; so are un jean and un short.
fact: un pull ("PUL") is a jumper, borrowed from the English word pullover and made shorter.
fact: une jupe ("ZHUP") is a skirt, une robe ("ROB") is a dress, une chemise ("shuh-MEEZ") is a shirt; a dressing gown is une robe de chambre.
fact: un chapeau ("sha-POH") is a hat and une casquette ("kass-KET") is a cap: -eau words are almost always masculine and -ette words almost always feminine.
fact: une veste is a jacket and des baskets are trainers; both are false friends.
fact: Most state schools in France have no uniform, and children wear their own clothes; school uniform is l'uniforme scolaire.
fact: des tongs ("TONG") are flip-flops, which Australians call thongs and New Zealanders call jandals; des sandales are sandals.
fact: des chaussures noires means black shoes: the noun takes s and the colour takes e (feminine) and s (plural).
fact: Mets ton manteau ! means "Put your coat on!"; mets comes from mettre, to put on; un manteau is a coat.
fact: un maillot de bain ("ma-YO duh BAN") is a swimsuit or swimming trunks; le maillot jaune is the yellow jersey in the Tour de France.
fact: rose takes an s with a plural noun: des chaussettes roses (pink socks); orange and marron never change.
fact: une écharpe ("ay-SHARP") is a scarf, des gants ("GAHN") are gloves and un bonnet ("bo-NAY") is a woolly hat; the t is silent in gants and bonnet.
fact: Slang here: "bruv" means mate; "bummer" (US, older) means what a shame; "ah'm no" (Scotland) means I'm not; "Ma" (Ireland) means Mum; "tamping" (Wales) means fuming; "you plum" (London) means you silly; "it's giving" (US Gen Z) means it looks good; "chur" (New Zealand) means great or thanks.
exercise: Max says "un pantalons" for one pair of trousers. What is right? | un pantalon | des pantalon | une pantalon | un pantalons
exercise: What is un pull? | a jumper | a swimming pool | a pull on a rope | a pair of trousers
exercise: What is une robe? | a dress | a dressing gown | a robot | a bathrobe
exercise: What is une chemise? | a shirt | a chemist | a nightie | a jacket
exercise: Ethan writes "Je porte une chapeau." What is right? | Je porte un chapeau. | Je porte une chapeau. | Je porte une chapelle. | Je porte un chapeaue.
exercise: Which word is almost certainly feminine? | casquette | chapeau | manteau | bateau
exercise: Hannah's mum asks, "Veste? Is that a vest?" What is une veste? | a jacket | a vest | a waistcoat for babies | a T-shirt
exercise: What are des baskets? | trainers | baskets for shopping | basketball hoops | sandals
exercise: Noah's penpal writes "On n'a pas ça !" about school uniform. Why? | Most French state schools have no uniform | French uniforms are secret | French children wear pyjamas to school | French schools only wear uniforms on Fridays
exercise: Isla says "J'aime mes jandals." What would a French friend call them? | des tongs | des jandals | des thongs | des sandales de plage d'Australie
exercise: Leo writes "des chaussure noir". What is right? | des chaussures noires | des chaussures noir | des chaussure noires | des chaussures noirs
exercise: What does Mets ton manteau ! mean? | Put your coat on! | Take your coat off! | Where's your coat? | That's my coat!
exercise: What is un maillot de bain? | a swimsuit | a mail bag | a bathroom towel | a bath toy
exercise: What is le maillot jaune? | the yellow jersey in the Tour de France | a yellow swimsuit worn at the beach in summer | a yellow mail bag | a yellow raincoat
exercise: Ava writes "des chaussettes rose". What is right? | des chaussettes roses | des chaussettes rose | des chaussette roses | des chaussettes rosees
exercise: Kai is cold. Which is right? | Je porte une écharpe et des gants. | Je porte un écharpe et des gants. | Je porte une écharpe et des gantes. | Je porte une écharpe et un gants.
exercise: How do you say gants? | "GAHN", the t is silent | "GANTS", with a clear t | "GANT", with a t | "JANTS"
test: Max says, "One trousers? That's mad, bruv." ("Bruv" means mate.) What's the truth? | French counts one pair as one thing: un pantalon | French has no word for trousers | Max is right: it should be des pantalons | Only shorts are singular in French
test: Poppy's gran smiles at un pull, "like we used to say". What's the story of the word? | It was borrowed from English pullover and shortened | It comes from pulling a jumper over your head in a race | It means "pool" because jumpers get wet | It comes from the old French word for sheep
test: Sophie's host mum holds up une jupe bleue. Sophie says, "Ah'm no wearing that in this weather but okay." ("Ah'm no" means I'm not.) What is she being offered? | a blue skirt | a blue dress | a blue shirt | a blue jumper
test: Ethan says, "Hats are boys? Bummer." ("Bummer" means what a shame.) Which tip helps guess the gender? | -eau words are almost always masculine | Every hat in French is feminine | Clothes for boys are masculine | Long words are always feminine
test: Hannah says, "No, it's a jacket, Ma." ("Ma" means Mum.) What could happen if her mum packs a vest instead? | Hannah would have no jacket for the cold | Hannah would have two jackets | Nothing, veste and vest are the same | Hannah would get a basket of trainers
test: Noah says "Lucky them" when his penpal has no uniform. What would be a good French question to ask her? | Qu'est-ce que tu portes à l'école ? | Où est ton maillot de bain ? | Quelle heure est-il ? | Tu as combien de chats et de chiens à la maison ?
test: Isla tries, "They're thongs, er, flip-flops." What should she say in French? | des tongs | des flip-flops | des thongs | des jandales
test: Leo says, "That's two letters for one mistake, tamping." ("Tamping" means fuming.) Why both e and s on noires? | chaussures is feminine and plural | noires is a different colour from noir | French always adds es to colours | The teacher added letters to be fussy
test: Aisha texts home, "She's bossing me about my coat." What does Mets ton manteau ! mean? | Put your coat on! | Sell your coat! | Your coat is dirty! | Your coat is too big!
test: Charlie's sister says, "It's swimming stuff, you plum." ("You plum" means you silly.) Where would you wear un maillot de bain? | at the swimming pool | to post a letter at the post office | at a wedding | in bed
test: Ava says, "Okay, it's giving, but why the s?" ("It's giving" means it looks good.) Which colour does NOT take an s? | orange | rose | noir | violet
test: ★★★ Kai's teacher says "Chur, perfect!" ("Chur" means great.) Which winter list has NO mistake? | une écharpe, des gants, un bonnet | un écharpe, des gants, un bonnet | une écharpe, des gant, une bonnet | une écharpe, des gants, une bonnet
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the noun pantalon? | It is singular in French, though its English equivalent is plural | It is invariably plural in French | It denotes a jacket in formal usage | It is feminine in the singular
exam: Identify the meaning of the French noun une veste. | A jacket | A vest | A dressing gown | A pair of trainers
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning school uniform in France. | The majority of state schools do not require one | All schools require a uniform of navy blue | Uniform is required solely on public holidays | Uniform is required solely in primary schools
exam: It is given that a plural feminine noun is qualified by the adjective noir. Determine the correct form. | noires | noir | noirs | noire
